# Without this, `core.autocrlf=true` (the Windows default) rewrites any PDF that
# git's heuristic reads as text — a PDF whose first 8000 bytes contain no NUL,
# e.g. one whose image streams are ASCII85-encoded. That silently changed two
# corpus documents' bytes and made the pinned hashes unreproducible.
*.pdf binary

# Committed OCR source artifacts are pinned by SHA-256 in
# packages/bank_statement_analysis/tests/corpus_cache/manifest.json and verified
# on every read. They are text, so without this they would check out with CRLF
# on Windows and hash differently there than in CI — the same defect as above,
# one layer up.
packages/bank_statement_analysis/tests/corpus_cache/ocr/*.md -text
# The other half of the same artifact — the intake page list — is pinned and
# verified the same way, so it needs the same protection.
packages/bank_statement_analysis/tests/corpus_cache/pages/*.json -text

# Other binary evidence and export formats.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.xlsx binary
*.zip binary
*.sh text eol=lf
